WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.844
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.960
SPEAKER: By now, you
probably understand

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.260
that Google customizes a lot
of our data center design.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.020
This customization traces back
to the beginning of a data

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.460
center story, where it lives.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.720
So how do we select
our data center sites?

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.970
Data center site
selection is all about

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.050
balancing a variety of factors.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.770
A data center location has
to have the right combination

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.890
of energy infrastructure,
proximity to our users,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.860
available workforce,
and available land.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.510
Some of our data
center campuses are

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.450
the size of hundreds of
American football fields.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.740
Yeah, that's a
lot of touchdowns.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.200
One of the first
things to assess

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.630
is exposure to
natural disasters.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.330
Areas that are prone to
earthquakes, extreme weather

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.360
events, or floods
aren't good candidates

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.400
to house thousands of machines.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.320
Though sometimes it's
unavoidable for what

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.390
we're looking for,
so we accommodate

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.560
through our designs.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.960
In case unexpected
scenarios arise,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.480
we've designed for redundant
power supplies and data

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.709
replication.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.350
Fiber network connectivity
is another factor.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.270
Understanding latency
limits and distance

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.360
to nearby points of presence,
or network access points,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.340
is a major piece of the puzzle.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.830
And since power is
a high ongoing cost

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.930
and impacts the
environment, we always

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.960
search for reliable and
efficient carbon-free energy

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.650
options to ensure sustainable
operations around the clock.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.500
Areas with temperate climates,
or other suitable environments,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.040
provide options for 100%
outdoor air or water

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.640
cooling to augment or replace
mechanical air conditioning.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.990
And add a cherry on top?

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.750
Finding sites with unique
geographic qualities

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.430
can improve environmental
performance.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.640
For example, our data
center in Belgium

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.700
uses recycled water from
a nearby industrial canal

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.430
to cool its servers,
making it more sustainable

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.040
and the first Google
data center to eliminate

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.480
refrigerated cooling.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.110
In Finland, we transformed an
old paper mill, located next

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.570
to the Bay of Finland,
into a data center.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.000
The bay's water,
sitting at 57 degrees

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.090
Fahrenheit or 14 degrees
Celsius all year round,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.630
allows us to use seawater
to cool the data center.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.120
Our Taiwan data center
is one of Asia's most

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.550
efficient and
environmentally-friendly.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.640
Our nighttime cooling and
thermal energy storage system

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.430
cools water at night when
temperatures are lower,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.890
storing it in large,
insulated tanks.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.470
Then during the day, it's
pumped throughout the facility

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.180
to cool our servers.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.450
Thanks in part to a partnership
with Dutch farmer cooperatives,

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.210
our data center in the
Netherlands sources carbon-free

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.910
power directly from
local wind farms.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.930
Our data center in Ireland uses
an advanced air cooling system

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.540
that takes advantage of
Ireland's temperate weather

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.700
to keep our servers
running smoothly.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.620
As a result, the
data center can be

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.840
cooled efficiently
with outdoor air,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.390
instead of air
conditioning units.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.390
As you can see, there's no
one-size-fits-all for Google

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.470
data centers.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.790
Each one is built to adapt to
local strengths and thrive.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.770
But what does this
mean for you at home

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.870
and our real-world applications?

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.430
Find out next time on
"Discovering Data Centers."

